<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lavaux</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lavaux is a region of terraced vineyards on the north shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and Vevey. The terraces were built from the 11th century under monasteries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Lausanne, who owned much of the land. Lavaux was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2007.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lavaux.jpg" alt="Lavaux" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lorenz Poffet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lavaux_Switzerland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lavaux is a region in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, in the district of Lavaux-Oron. It consists of 830 hectares of terraced vineyards that stretch for about 30 km along the south-facing northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>. The main wine grape grown here is the Chasselas. The terraced vineyards date back to the 11th century, when the land was under the rule of Benedictine and Cistercian monasteries. Many hikes are possible through the vineyards, including a trail called the Terrasses de Lavaux, which runs from Saint-Saphorin to Lutry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 20th century the vineyards became the subject of popular votes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>. In 1977 the voters of the canton accepted, with 55 per cent, a popular initiative called Save Lavaux, and in 1979 a law was made to protect the region, the Loi sur le plan de protection de Lavaux. Under cantonal law the vineyards are protected from development.</p><p>When the new cantonal constitution came into force in 2003 it did not contain the clause protecting Lavaux. A second Save Lavaux initiative, launched to restore it, was accepted in 2005 by 81 per cent of voters. A third, launched in 2009 by Franz Weber to reduce the opportunities for new construction, was rejected on 19 May 2014 by 68 per cent of voters, while the counter-proposal of the Council of State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, which strengthened the protection less strictly, was accepted by the same share.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since July 2007 Lavaux has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> under the formal name Lavaux, Vineyard Terraces. The region also appears in popular culture: Prince named a song Lavaux on his album 20Ten, released in 2010, and in 2011 the Swiss Post issued three special stamps for it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lavaux” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prince-bishops ruled Lausanne and estates such as <b>Lavaux</b>, famous for vineyards, while the counts of Savoy dominated the rest of Vaud.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-lausanne">Prince-Bishopric of Lausanne</a></li></ul></section>
